<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Florence Nightingale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a>–<a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as The Lady with the Lamp</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> Nightingale was an English social reformer and statistician, regarded as the founder of modern nursing. Born in Florence, she came to prominence during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, arriving at the Selimiye Barracks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scutari</a> in November 1854 to manage nurses caring for wounded British soldiers, and reduced death rates by improving hygiene. In 1860 she established a nursing school at St Thomas' Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the first secular nursing school in the world. She wrote Notes on Nursing (1859) and presented her analysis in graphical forms such as the polar area diagram.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florence-nightingale.jpg" alt="Florence Nightingale" width="330" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Hering (1814-1893) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Florence_Nightingale_(H_Hering_NPG_x82368).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Battlefield reporting and <b>Florence Nightingale</b>'s nursing made it a turning point in public views of war.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-28">28 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Florence Nightingale</b>, named after her birthplace, was born to a wealthy English family.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-12">12 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If a patient is cold, if a patient is feverish, if a patient is faint, if he is sick after taking food, if he has a bed-sore, it is generally the fault not of the disease, but of the nursing.&#8221;</p><footer>Notes on Nursing (1860), Introduction &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Notes_on_Nursing:_What_It_Is,_and_What_It_Is_Not/Introduction" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It has been said and written scores of times, that every woman makes a good nurse. I believe, on the contrary, that the very elements of nursing are all but unknown.&#8221;</p><footer>Notes on Nursing (1860), Introduction &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Notes_on_Nursing:_What_It_Is,_and_What_It_Is_Not/Introduction" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But you want to do the thing that is good, whether it is &quot;suitable for a woman&quot; or not.&#8221;</p><footer>Notes on Nursing (1860), Conclusion &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Notes_on_Nursing:_What_It_Is,_and_What_It_Is_Not/Conclusion" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
